From efd85475b49b30a7569680a15b11ce76ea8615ed Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Giuseppe Maggio <jertlok@proton.me>
Date: Sat, 19 Sep 2026 15:17:49 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 240/240] arm64: dts: qcom: msm8998: take cpu capacity off the
 vendor's own scale

capacity-dmips-mhz was 1024 silver / 1536 gold, which normalises to
cpu_capacity 549/1024 for the A53 cluster. The vendor's own kernel arrives at
419/1024, and it does not get there from a property we can copy across.

Downstream msm8998.dtsi:51-228 carries efficiency = <1024> on the silver cores
and <1536> on the gold ones -- the same pair, which is where ours came from.
But arch/arm64/kernel/topology.c:297-306 does not use it for capacity:

	static void update_cpu_capacity(unsigned int cpu)
	{
		unsigned long capacity = SCHED_CAPACITY_SCALE;

		if (cpu_core_energy(cpu)) {
			int max_cap_idx = cpu_core_energy(cpu)->nr_cap_states - 1;
			capacity = cpu_core_energy(cpu)->cap_states[max_cap_idx].cap;
		}

		set_capacity_scale(cpu, capacity);
	}

The last cap state of the energy model is what becomes cpu_scale. efficiency
feeds per_cpu(cpu_efficiency) at :226-230 and is read back by
arch_get_cpu_efficiency(), which is a different consumer. So the vendor's
capacity is msm8998.dtsi:282-350 -- CPU_COST_0 busy-cost-data ending at
419 201, CPU_COST_1 ending at 1024 1683 -- and it is 419/1024.

Per MHz that is A53:A73 = 0.508 against the 0.666 the efficiency pair implies.
We were overstating the little cluster by 31%.

This matters more than it used to. The OPP tables just gained opp-microwatt
derived from those same two cost curves, so the energy model and the capacity
scale are now read together by every EAS placement decision, and they have to
come off the same measurement -- which, downstream, they literally do: one
array feeds both. Overstating silver capacity while costing it from that curve
tells the scheduler a little core can absorb work it cannot, at a power figure
that assumed it could not.

Expressed as 521/1024 rather than 781/1536 so the reference 1024 sits on the
biggest core, as it does elsewhere in arm64. The ratio is what is read:
521 * 1900800 / (1024 * 2361600) = 0.40925, i.e. 419/1024.

Assisted-by: LLM
Signed-off-by: Giuseppe Maggio <jertlok@proton.me>
---
 arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/msm8998.dtsi | 27 +++++++++++++++++++--------
 1 file changed, 19 insertions(+), 8 deletions(-)

diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/msm8998.dtsi b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/msm8998.dtsi
index 04631d4b8da2..162df5a90788 100644
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/msm8998.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/msm8998.dtsi
@@ -150,12 +150,23 @@ cpus {
 		#address-cells = <2>;
 		#size-cells = <0>;
 
+		/*
+		 * capacity-dmips-mhz: 521 silver against 1024 gold puts
+		 * cpu_capacity at 419/1024 for the A53s. That is the vendor's
+		 * number, but not one of its properties: downstream carries
+		 * efficiency = <1024>/<1536> (the pair this replaces, worth
+		 * 549), and arch/arm64/kernel/topology.c:297 ignores it for
+		 * capacity, taking cpu_scale from the last cap state of the
+		 * energy model instead -- CPU_COST_0 ends at 419, CPU_COST_1
+		 * at 1024. Since our OPPs now carry opp-microwatt from those
+		 * same two curves, capacity has to be read off the same scale.
+		 */
 		cpu0: cpu@0 {
 			device_type = "cpu";
 			compatible = "qcom,kryo280";
 			reg = <0x0 0x0>;
 			enable-method = "psci";
-			capacity-dmips-mhz = <1024>;
+			capacity-dmips-mhz = <521>;
 			cpu-idle-states = <&little_cpu_sleep_0 &little_cpu_sleep_1
 					  &system_sleep>;
 			next-level-cache = <&l2_0>;
@@ -176,7 +187,7 @@ cpu1: cpu@1 {
 			compatible = "qcom,kryo280";
 			reg = <0x0 0x1>;
 			enable-method = "psci";
-			capacity-dmips-mhz = <1024>;
+			capacity-dmips-mhz = <521>;
 			cpu-idle-states = <&little_cpu_sleep_0 &little_cpu_sleep_1
 					  &system_sleep>;
 			next-level-cache = <&l2_0>;
@@ -192,7 +203,7 @@ cpu2: cpu@2 {
 			compatible = "qcom,kryo280";
 			reg = <0x0 0x2>;
 			enable-method = "psci";
-			capacity-dmips-mhz = <1024>;
+			capacity-dmips-mhz = <521>;
 			cpu-idle-states = <&little_cpu_sleep_0 &little_cpu_sleep_1
 					  &system_sleep>;
 			next-level-cache = <&l2_0>;
@@ -208,7 +219,7 @@ cpu3: cpu@3 {
 			compatible = "qcom,kryo280";
 			reg = <0x0 0x3>;
 			enable-method = "psci";
-			capacity-dmips-mhz = <1024>;
+			capacity-dmips-mhz = <521>;
 			cpu-idle-states = <&little_cpu_sleep_0 &little_cpu_sleep_1
 					  &system_sleep>;
 			next-level-cache = <&l2_0>;
@@ -224,7 +235,7 @@ cpu4: cpu@100 {
 			compatible = "qcom,kryo280";
 			reg = <0x0 0x100>;
 			enable-method = "psci";
-			capacity-dmips-mhz = <1536>;
+			capacity-dmips-mhz = <1024>;
 			cpu-idle-states = <&big_cpu_sleep_0 &big_cpu_sleep_1
 					  &system_sleep>;
 			next-level-cache = <&l2_1>;
@@ -245,7 +256,7 @@ cpu5: cpu@101 {
 			compatible = "qcom,kryo280";
 			reg = <0x0 0x101>;
 			enable-method = "psci";
-			capacity-dmips-mhz = <1536>;
+			capacity-dmips-mhz = <1024>;
 			cpu-idle-states = <&big_cpu_sleep_0 &big_cpu_sleep_1
 					  &system_sleep>;
 			next-level-cache = <&l2_1>;
@@ -261,7 +272,7 @@ cpu6: cpu@102 {
 			compatible = "qcom,kryo280";
 			reg = <0x0 0x102>;
 			enable-method = "psci";
-			capacity-dmips-mhz = <1536>;
+			capacity-dmips-mhz = <1024>;
 			cpu-idle-states = <&big_cpu_sleep_0 &big_cpu_sleep_1
 					  &system_sleep>;
 			next-level-cache = <&l2_1>;
@@ -277,7 +288,7 @@ cpu7: cpu@103 {
 			compatible = "qcom,kryo280";
 			reg = <0x0 0x103>;
 			enable-method = "psci";
-			capacity-dmips-mhz = <1536>;
+			capacity-dmips-mhz = <1024>;
 			cpu-idle-states = <&big_cpu_sleep_0 &big_cpu_sleep_1
 					  &system_sleep>;
 			next-level-cache = <&l2_1>;
